WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.400
A server needs power, a way to reject heat and communication.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.100
Moving it into orbit changes the paths supporting all three functions.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:22.000
The question is not just whether it works, but which task justifies it.

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.400
On Earth, air or water can carry heat away.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.100
In the outside vacuum, no air flows through the server.

6
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:44.000
Heat must reach a surface that radiates it into space.

7
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.400
Above the atmosphere, a panel avoids atmospheric absorption.

8
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:58.100
But an orbit can cross Earth’s shadow and lose sunlight.

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:06.000
Compare useful energy over the mission, not just power in sunlight.

10
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:13.400
An orbital sensor can produce more data than it can send.

11
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.100
When the task allows it, process onboard and transmit a smaller result.

12
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:28.000
The reduction must preserve the information needed for that task.

13
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:35.400
Running one device demonstrates that test under its conditions.

14
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:42.100
More devices also need more power, heat rejection and communication.

15
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:50.000
Reliability at a larger scale needs evidence of its own.

16
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:57.400
Hold the task and required service level constant.

17
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:04.100
Include power, cooling, links, launch and end of life.

18
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:12.000
One local advantage does not prove that the whole system is better.

